course: money
lesson: 12
title: Offers: 3 for 2, buy one get one free, bundles
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Shops put up offers to make you buy more: "3 for 2" means you take 3 and pay for 2, and "buy one get one free" (BOGOF) means two for the price of one.
explain: An offer only saves money if you would use everything: shampoo at £3 a bottle on 3 for 2 costs £6 for three, which is £2 a bottle, but if you only need one, the offer makes you spend £3 more.
explain: Some "deals" are not deals at all: cereal at £2.40 a box costs £4.80 for two, so a "2 for £5" sign makes you pay 20p more, and shops count on people not checking.
explain: Before you grab an offer, do the sum: what would I pay normally, what do I pay with the offer, and do I really need all of it?
fact: "3 for 2" means you get 3 items but pay for only 2.
fact: "Buy one get one free" (BOGOF) means you get 2 items for the price of 1.
fact: An offer saves money only if you need everything and use it before it goes off.
fact: A use-by date is the last day food is safe to eat.
fact: Some offers cost more than buying the items normally, so always do the sum.
fact: Half price means you pay half: divide the price by 2.
fact: Bigger packs are often cheaper for each item, but you spend more in total.
fact: A loyalty card gives you a reward, like a free drink, for coming back often; it only helps if you would shop there anyway.
fact: A "spend £20, get £5 off" deal pushes you to spend more; it helps only if the extra thing is useful.
fact: A bundle is two or more things sold together for less than buying them one by one.
fact: Percent (%) means "out of 100"; 20% extra on a 100 g bag is 20 g more.
fact: A "was" price only shows a real saving if the old price was really charged for a good while.
exercise: Shampoo is £3 a bottle on "3 for 2". How much do 3 bottles cost? | £6 | £9 | £3 | £4.50
exercise: With "3 for 2" on £3 bottles, what does each bottle really cost? | £2 | £3 | £1 | £1.50
exercise: Mum only needs 1 bottle of £3 shampoo. How much more would the 3 for 2 deal make her spend? | £3 | £6 | £2 | £0
exercise: What does BOGOF mean? | Buy one get one free | Buy only good offers first | Bring old goods or food | Bag of gifts on Fridays
exercise: Yoghurts are £1 each on BOGOF, and the second one goes off. How much did Theo really save? | Nothing | £1 | 50p | £2
exercise: Cereal is £2.40 a box. What do 2 boxes cost normally? | £4.80 | £4.40 | £5.00 | £4.20
exercise: How much more does the "2 for £5" deal cost than 2 boxes at £2.40? | 20p | 40p | 60p | £1
exercise: A toy was £16 and is now half price. What does it cost? | £8 | £6 | £4 | £12
exercise: "4 lollies for £1." What does each lolly cost on the deal? | 25p | 20p | 30p | 40p
exercise: One lolly alone is 30p. What do 2 single lollies cost? | 60p | 50p | £1 | 75p
exercise: Mum's coffee is £3, and 10 stamps get one free. How much does she spend to earn the free drink? | £30 | £33 | £27 | £10
exercise: Dad's basket is £17. The deal is "Spend £20, get £5 off." If he adds a £3 item, what does he pay? | £15 | £20 | £12 | £17
exercise: "Was £10, now £7." How much is saved, if the old price was real? | £3 | £7 | £17 | £70
exercise: Kit is £30 and boots are £25. What do they cost bought separately? | £55 | £50 | £45 | £5
exercise: The kit and boots bundle is £50. How much does Harry save if he needs both? | £5 | £10 | £55 | £25
exercise: A 100 g bag of crisps gets "20% extra free". How much is in the bag now? | 120 g | 200 g | 102 g | 80 g
exercise: When is a loyalty card a good thing? | When you would shop there anyway | When it makes you go somewhere more expensive | Always, because it's free | Never, because rewards are fake
test: Theo grabs BOGOF yoghurts and Dad says "free? wasted, more like" when one goes off. What's the lesson? | A deal is only good if you use it all | Free things never go off | Buy four next time to be safe | Yoghurts should be kept in the cupboard
test: Lucy: "wait, 2 for £5, but they're £2.40 each — that's MORE expensive!" Is she right? | Yes, 2 × £2.40 is £4.80, 20p less than the deal | No, the deal saves 20p | No, they cost exactly the same | Yes, the deal costs £1 more
test: Rory goes "grab something else, Dad!" for spend £20, get £5 off. Basket's £17. Dad adds £3 of bin bags they needed anyway. Is it a good move? | Yes: he pays £15 for £20 of useful stuff | No: he pays £20 instead of £17 | No: he pays £25 now | Yes: everything becomes free
test: Aoife wants 2 lollies. Mate says "get 4 for £1, it's cheaper, innit." What's the full story? | Each is cheaper, but she spends £1 instead of 60p | 4 for £1 is cheaper in total too | 2 lollies cost £1 anyway | Singles are always cheaper
test: What would happen if shops put "3 for 2" on milk that goes off in 2 days, and a small family bought it? | They'd likely throw some away, so no real saving | They'd save twice as much money | The milk would last longer | They'd get the third pint for free forever
test: Gemma: "was £10, now £7, so I save three quid, sorted." Her mum, an old-school Gen X mum, says "don't have a cow, check the was price first." ("Don't have a cow" means calm down.) Why? | The "was" price might only have been charged for a few days | £10 minus £7 is not £3 | Shops are never allowed to cut prices | The "now" price is always fake
test: Harry: "bundle £50, kit £30, boots £25 — I save a fiver!" But he already has boots. Does he save? | No, he'd pay £50 instead of £30 for the kit alone | Yes, he saves exactly £5 | Yes, he saves £25 | No, he'd pay £55
test: A toy is "half price!" and was £24. Your mate says "so it's £14." Is he right? | No, half of £24 is £12 | Yes, half of £24 is £14 | No, it's £20 | Yes, half price means £10 off
test: Coffee-shop stamp card: Mum says "they want you to keep coming back, that's the trick." What does the shop get? | Customers who return again and again | Nothing, the free drink costs them a fortune | Mum's bank details | A free coffee for the shop
test: Big bag of crisps: "20% extra free!" Ruby's bro goes "so it's double, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) What's true for a 100 g bag? | It's 120 g, only a bit more | It's 200 g, twice as much | It's 80 g, a bit less | It's 102 g
test: Shampoo is £3 on "3 for 2". Mum needs 3 bottles anyway this month. How much does she save? | £3 | £6 | £2 | £9
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an offer designated "three for the price of two"? | Three articles are supplied whilst payment is made in respect of two only | Two articles are supplied whilst payment is made in respect of three | Three articles are supplied without any payment whatsoever | The price of every article is reduced by two pounds
exam: It is given that a cereal is priced at two pounds forty pence per box and is offered at two boxes for five pounds. Ascertain the excess payable under the offer. | Twenty pence | Forty pence | Sixty pence | One pound
exam: It is given that an article formerly priced at sixteen pounds is reduced to half price. Determine the reduced price. | Eight pounds | Six pounds | Four pounds | Twelve pounds
exam: Identify the principal condition upon which a multiple-purchase offer occasions a genuine saving. | That every article so purchased is required and used | That the articles are perishable and expire promptly | That the purchaser possesses a loyalty card | That the offer is displayed in large lettering
